/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 415 0 R
/E (Also, the author for the [Pou24a], provides a comprehensive theoretical foundation, clearly explaining core Kubernetes concepts such as pods, services, deployments, and orchestration principles in [Pou24b] book. While Kubernetes is powerful for production environments, it introduces unnecessary complexity for GNS3 network simulations. GNS3 has built-in support for Docker containers, making it an ideal choice for lightweight network topology. Unlike Kubernetes, which requires a control plane, API server, and complex networking configurations, Docker containers in GNS3 can be deployed instantly, consuming fewer system resources. Additionally, Kubernetes' orchestration features - such as self-healing, autoscaling, and service discovery - are not needed for a controlled network simulation environment where all nodes are predefined. )
/Pg 30 0 R
/K [8]
>>
endobj
920 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 415 0 R
/E (With the growing demand for high-quality, uninterrupted network services, service providers need to ensure the reliability and performance of their networks. One challenge is to accurately measure and predict service quality, which often involves monitoring many variables. The article [Tur19] introduces a novel approach for network service management by modeling the network topology as a Finite State Machine \(FSM\). The paper leverages machine learning \(ML\) classification algorithms to classify network states based on Key Performance Indicators \(KPIs\) that are directly observed from network elements. The goal is to detect service degradation and identify its root causes using only measurable network data. The authors implement the approach in a simulated network environment using GNS3, performing experiments to assess the accuracy of different ML algorithms in classifying network states. The study also highlights the key KPIs that most impact service quality, with the results showing that ensemble learning methods like Gradient Boosting can achieve up to 88% accuracy in classifying network topology state )
/Pg 30 0 R
/K [9]
>>
endobj
921 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 415 0 R
/E (In [Alb22], the authors review various experimentation environments for data center routing protocols, highlighting the need for scalable and realistic testing platforms. Among the tools analyzed, GNS3 is a strong candidate for experimentation and learning. GNS3 provides an intuitive graphical user interface \(GUI\), simplifying the design and management of network topologies, which is especially useful for controlled, small-to-medium-scale experiments. Unlike some other tools, it allows for the emulation of real network images, enabling testing with actual router software images rather than relying solely on theoretical models. Additionally, while its Docker integration is not as extensive as in other platforms, it still enables container-based networking experiments, expanding its versatility. The paper also discusses data center routing protocols and the challenges of emulating large-scale networks, reinforcing the value of GNS3 as )
/Pg 30 0 R
/K [10]
>>
endobj
