/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/P 416 0 R
/T (The study [Emm23] proposes a method for integrating machine learning into SD-WAN using a GNS3-based testbed. A decision-tree model, trained on traffic features such as protocol and flow duration, is used to classify packets and adapt routing strategies in real time. The simulation leverages Python automation and demonstrates how traffic classification improves bandwidth usage, anomaly detection, and overall network responsiveness. Although focused on SD-WAN, the architecture translates well to container-based test environments. It confirms the feasibility of lightweight AI integration in virtual networks like GNS3 and supports the idea that traffic behavior can be dynamically analyzed using real-time metrics. The study emphasizes that even with limited resources, ML can significantly enhance monitoring precision, especially when supported by flexible, emulated infrastructures. )
/E (The study [Emm23] proposes a method for integrating machine learning into SD-WAN using a GNS3-based testbed. A decision-tree model, trained on traffic features such as protocol and flow duration, is used to classify packets and adapt routing strategies in real time. The simulation leverages Python automation and demonstrates how traffic classification improves bandwidth usage, anomaly detection, and overall network responsiveness. Although focused on SD-WAN, the architecture translates well to container-based test environments. It confirms the feasibility of lightweight AI integration in virtual networks like GNS3 and supports the idea that traffic behavior can be dynamically analyzed using real-time metrics. The study emphasizes that even with limited resources, ML can significantly enhance monitoring precision, especially when supported by flexible, emulated infrastructures. )
/Pg 31 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
930 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/P 416 0 R
/T (The paper [Sal24] explores the application of machine learning techniques for classifying network traffic within a software-defined networking \(SDN\) environment. Supervised and unsupervised models such as Decision Tree and K-Means are used to distinguish traffic types like Domain Name System \(DNS\), Internet Control Message Protocol \(ICMP\), and Hypertext Transfer Protocol \(HTTP\). Despite the SDN context, the data handling methods and ML model structure are highly relevant for container-based architectures. The study highlights how accurate results can be obtained with limited features and lightweight models. This aligns with the goals of the present project, where containers simulate diverse traffic types. The research supports the use of real-time classification techniques for efficient traffic monitoring in virtual environments \(GNS3\). )
/E (The paper [Sal24] explores the application of machine learning techniques for classifying network traffic within a software-defined networking \(SDN\) environment. Supervised and unsupervised models such as Decision Tree and K-Means are used to distinguish traffic types like Domain Name System \(DNS\), Internet Control Message Protocol \(ICMP\), and Hypertext Transfer Protocol \(HTTP\). Despite the SDN context, the data handling methods and ML model structure are highly relevant for container-based architectures. The study highlights how accurate results can be obtained with limited features and lightweight models. This aligns with the goals of the present project, where containers simulate diverse traffic types. The research supports the use of real-time classification techniques for efficient traffic monitoring in virtual environments \(GNS3\). )
/Pg 31 0 R
/K [8]
>>
endobj
931 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/P 416 0 R
/T (The study [Lyp24] focuses on comparing various feature extraction tools for preparing network traffic data for ML classification. Through testing different pipelines, the authors demonstrate how small changes in preprocessing significantly affect model performance. The study is especially relevant to this project, where metrics from Node Exporter and SNMP must be preprocessed before AI analysis. It highlights the critical role of choosing the right features and ensuring data consistency. Tools that generate clean and relevant features lead to more accurate predictions and reduce false positives in anomaly detection. This underlines the importance of preprocessing in building AI-based monitoring systems. )
/E (The study [Lyp24] focuses on comparing various feature extraction tools for preparing network traffic data for ML classification. Through testing different pipelines, the authors demonstrate how small changes in preprocessing significantly affect model performance. The study is especially relevant to this project, where metrics from Node Exporter and SNMP must be preprocessed before AI analysis. It highlights the critical role of choosing the right features and ensuring data consistency. Tools that generate clean and relevant features lead to more accurate predictions and reduce false positives in anomaly detection. This underlines the importance of preprocessing in building AI-based monitoring systems. )
/Pg 31 0 R
/K [9]
>>
