<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 418 0 R
/E (3.1 Anomaly Detection )
/Pg 33 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
1016 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 418 0 R
/E (Anomaly detection is the process of finding unusual patterns or values in data that do not match expected behavior. These anomalies, also called outliers, often indicate problems such as failures, attacks, or errors. Because of this, anomaly detection is essential in monitoring networks, systems, or services in real-time environments, where quick response is needed. Traditional rule-based or statistical methods often fail to keep up with modern data, which is complex, high-volume, and continuously evolving. As a result, more flexible and scalable techniques are needed to detect anomalies reliably in these conditions, especially in dynamic or unpredictable networks. )
/Pg 33 0 R
/K [8]
>>
endobj
1017 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 418 0 R
/E (One such method is the Isolation Forest \(IF\) algorithm, which identifies anomalies by separating data points through random partitioning. The key idea is that anomalies are rare and different, so they can be isolated more quickly than normal data. The algorithm builds multiple trees and calculates how deep each data point appears. Anomalies usually lie closer to the root, requiring fewer splits. This makes the method fast and memory-efficient, and suitable even when no labeled anomalies are available. Its performance holds up well in dynamic data environments with multiple variables and real-world variations in behavior [Xia18]. )
/Pg 33 0 R
/K [9]
>>
endobj
1018 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 418 0 R
/E (Supervised machine learning models such as Decision Trees, Random Forests, SVMs, and k- Nearest Neighbors are also used when labeled data is available. These models learn to distinguish between normal and abnormal behavior based on past examples. Their accuracy improves when irrelevant or redundant features are removed. Genetic Algorithms help by selecting optimal inputs through evolutionary processes, while Correlation-Based Selection keeps only features that strongly relate to the target output. This makes the models more efficient, especially in large and noisy datasets where complexity can affect performance [Alt24]. )
/Pg 33 0 R
/K [10]
>>
endobj
1019 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 418 0 R
/E (Deep learning models like Convolutional Neural Networks \(CNN\) provide another approach by automatically extracting useful features from raw data. CNNs process inputs through multiple layers that capture both simple and complex patterns, making them well suited for detecting anomalies in structured data such as images or time series. They can operate in both supervised and unsupervised scenarios, offering flexibility in various applications. CNNs are especially powerful in visual or sequential data, where manual feature extraction is difficult [Jai24]. )
/Pg 33 0 R
/K [11]
>>
endobj
1020 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 418 0 R
/E (Autoencoders are another unsupervised method. They aim to reconstruct the input data, and if the reconstruction fails, it suggests the presence of unfamiliar or abnormal data. Likewise, Generative Adversarial Networks \(GAN\), composed of a generator and a discriminator, identify anomalies by comparing new inputs with the learned distribution of normal data. These models are especially useful in domains where anomalies are subtle or hard to define clearly, such as medical diagnostics or manufacturing inspection, and where labeled data is difficult to obtain [Jai24]. )
/Pg 33 0 R
/K [12]
>>
endobj
1021 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 418 0 R
/E (Choosing the right method depends on the data and context. Isolation Forest, being fast and capable of handling unlabeled data, is suitable in many practical scenarios. While classical models require clean labels and deep learning needs more resources, Isolation Forest often offers a more efficient and adaptable solution. It strikes a balance between simplicity, performance, and real- time application needs, making it effective in a wide range of monitoring systems and environments. )
/Pg 33 0 R
/K [13]
>>
endobj
