/T (  3.5 GNS3  In the field of networking, the ability to test and configure network topologies as if they were physical systems, is a major advantage. It helps reduce costs and makes learning or testing much easier and more flexible. Instead of requiring physical routers, switches, and other equipment, nearly everything can be simulated on a computer. This approach is especially useful for students, engineers, and researchers who aim to build and experiment with networks without relying on a physical lab [Fon18]. )
/E (  3.5 GNS3  In the field of networking, the ability to test and configure network topologies as if they were physical systems, is a major advantage. It helps reduce costs and makes learning or testing much easier and more flexible. Instead of requiring physical routers, switches, and other equipment, nearly everything can be simulated on a computer. This approach is especially useful for students, engineers, and researchers who aim to build and experiment with networks without relying on a physical lab [Fon18]. )
/Pg 39 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
1126 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/P 424 0 R
/T (GNS3 \(Graphical Network Simulator 3\) represents one of the most powerful tools available for this purpose. It enables the creation of virtual network topologies that behave very similarly to real-world networks. This makes the tool ideal for training, practicing configurations, testing new designs, and simulating failures or network issues [Fon18]. )
/E (GNS3 \(Graphical Network Simulator 3\) represents one of the most powerful tools available for this purpose. It enables the creation of virtual network topologies that behave very similarly to real-world networks. This makes the tool ideal for training, practicing configurations, testing new designs, and simulating failures or network issues [Fon18]. )
/Pg 39 0 R
/K [8]
>>
endobj
1127 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/P 424 0 R
/T (GNS3 is composed of two main components: a graphical user interface \(GUI\) and a backend server. GUI provides a visual environment where network designs are created by dragging and dropping devices like routers, switches, and computers onto a workspace, then linking them as if using physical cables. The backend server handles the execution of these devices, running their operating systems \(OS\) and managing networking tasks to ensure realistic simulation behavior. )
/E (GNS3 is composed of two main components: a graphical user interface \(GUI\) and a backend server. GUI provides a visual environment where network designs are created by dragging and dropping devices like routers, switches, and computers onto a workspace, then linking them as if using physical cables. The backend server handles the execution of these devices, running their operating systems \(OS\) and managing networking tasks to ensure realistic simulation behavior. )
/Pg 39 0 R
/K [9]
>>
endobj
1128 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/P 424 0 R
/T (One of the key advantages of GNS3 is its ability to emulate actual device operating systems, not just simulate their behavior. This means that real firmware images from vendors such as Cisco, Juniper, or MikroTik can be used. By running these images, users gain access to the same command-line interface and features found on real devices. This makes GNS3 far more accurate and valuable than basic simulators [Fon18]. )
/E (One of the key advantages of GNS3 is its ability to emulate actual device operating systems, not just simulate their behavior. This means that real firmware images from vendors such as Cisco, Juniper, or MikroTik can be used. By running these images, users gain access to the same command-line interface and features found on real devices. This makes GNS3 far more accurate and valuable than basic simulators [Fon18]. )
/Pg 39 0 R
/K [10]
>>
endobj
1129 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/P 424 0 R
/T (Figure 5 shows the GNS3 interface, where users build and interact with virtual networks. A new project is created, and virtual devices such as routers, switches, firewalls, or end-user machines are added. These devices are connected by drawing links between them. Each device can be accessed through a console window, where configuration commands are entered, like working with real hardware in a lab environment. )
/E (Figure 5 shows the GNS3 interface, where users build and interact with virtual networks. A new project is created, and virtual devices such as routers, switches, firewalls, or end-user machines are added. These devices are connected by drawing links between them. Each device can be accessed through a console window, where configuration commands are entered, like working with real hardware in a lab environment. )
/Pg 39 0 R
/K [11]
>>
