/T (  3.8 SNMP and Node Exporter for Scrape Metrics  Prometheus relies on exporters to collect metrics from external systems. Two common types of exporters used in monitoring infrastructure components are SNMP Exporter and Node Exporter. Both act as bridges between Prometheus and the devices or systems being monitored, exposing structured metrics in a format that Prometheus can scrape over HTTP. )
/E (  3.8 SNMP and Node Exporter for Scrape Metrics  Prometheus relies on exporters to collect metrics from external systems. Two common types of exporters used in monitoring infrastructure components are SNMP Exporter and Node Exporter. Both act as bridges between Prometheus and the devices or systems being monitored, exposing structured metrics in a format that Prometheus can scrape over HTTP. )
/Pg 44 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
1198 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/P 429 0 R
/T (SNMP Exporter - the SNMP Exporter is designed to retrieve data from network devices that support the Simple Network Management Protocol \(SNMP\), such as routers, switches, and firewalls. SNMP works over User Datagram Protocol \(UDP\) and uses structured identifiers \(OIDs\) to expose data like interface traffic, error rates, and device status. An OID is a numerical identifier that uniquely references a specific metric or variable exposed by a device [OID25]. These identifiers follow a tree-like structure defined in standardized Management Information Bases \(MIBs\). For example, the OID .1.3.6.1.2.1.2.2.1.10 refers to ifInOctets, which represents the number of bytes received on a particular interface. Similarly, .1.3.6.1.2.1.2.2.1.16 refers to ifOutOctets, for outgoing bytes. The SNMP Exporter uses a configuration file \(.yml file\) to map human-readable metric names to these OIDs. )
/E (SNMP Exporter - the SNMP Exporter is designed to retrieve data from network devices that support the Simple Network Management Protocol \(SNMP\), such as routers, switches, and firewalls. SNMP works over User Datagram Protocol \(UDP\) and uses structured identifiers \(OIDs\) to expose data like interface traffic, error rates, and device status. An OID is a numerical identifier that uniquely references a specific metric or variable exposed by a device [OID25]. These identifiers follow a tree-like structure defined in standardized Management Information Bases \(MIBs\). For example, the OID .1.3.6.1.2.1.2.2.1.10 refers to ifInOctets, which represents the number of bytes received on a particular interface. Similarly, .1.3.6.1.2.1.2.2.1.16 refers to ifOutOctets, for outgoing bytes. The SNMP Exporter uses a configuration file \(.yml file\) to map human-readable metric names to these OIDs. )
/Pg 44 0 R
/K [8]
>>
endobj
1199 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/P 429 0 R
/T (When Prometheus initiates a scrape, it sends an HTTP request to the SNMP Exporter. The exporter then performs an SNMP query to the device \(usually on port 161/UDP\), retrieves the relevant OID values, and converts them into Prometheus-compatible metrics [Pro25]. This allows Prometheus to monitor network devices in the same way it monitors systems that provide data over HTTP. )
/E (When Prometheus initiates a scrape, it sends an HTTP request to the SNMP Exporter. The exporter then performs an SNMP query to the device \(usually on port 161/UDP\), retrieves the relevant OID values, and converts them into Prometheus-compatible metrics [Pro25]. This allows Prometheus to monitor network devices in the same way it monitors systems that provide data over HTTP. )
/Pg 44 0 R
/K [9]
>>
endobj
