1230 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 430 0 R
/E (This chapter presents how AI is used to predict traffic and detect anomalies in a basic network topology implemented in GNS3 using Docker containers as endpoints to generate traffic. A preliminary design was presented in [Cir25] at SSET 2025. To be more precise, the topology includes four Docker containers and each one produces a different type of traffic: Idle container does not receive or transmit traffic, a Ping container sends ICMP request at fixed intervals, a TCP/HTTP container that uses a browser to generates TCP and HTTP traffic and an Anomaly container design to simulate anomalous traffic, such as a ping flood. All these containers are connected to a router through a switch, forming the main components of the network topology. The router is further connected to the host PC via a Cloud node to provide Internet connectivity and access to each container individually for data collection and monitoring purposes. Figure X offers a visualization of this topology from GNS3, illustrating the relationships between the network components. Traffic prediction is performed using an LSTM model, which is well-suited for capturing temporal patterns, while anomalies are detected using an Isolation Forest model, known for its efficiency in identifying outliers in multidimensional data. )
/Pg 45 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
1231 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 430 0 R
/E (To collect network metrics from the GNS3 topology, Prometheus gathers data from monitoring services running on the containers and the router. This data is stored and made available for visualization, with Grafana regularly requesting metrics from Prometheus to display them as graphs and dashboards. Additionally, AI scripts analyze the collected data to provide predictions and detect anomalies, sending their results back to Prometheus 3.3.0 for integrated visualization in Grafana 12.0. )
/Pg 45 0 R
/K [8]
>>
endobj
1232 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 430 0 R
/E (The GNS3 installer is available on the official website and supports all major operating systems, including Windows, macOS, and Linux [GNS25]. In this thesis, this emulator was installed on a Windows 11 system because it offered good compatibility with both the client and the virtualization tools needed. )
/Pg 45 0 R
/K [9]
>>
endobj
1233 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 430 0 R
/E (During the installation process, it was important to check the option to install the GNS3 VM. This is a lightweight Virtual Machine \(VM\), based on Ubuntu 20.04.6.LTS Linux Operating System, that was specially created to work with GNS3. The purpose of the VM is to improve performance and simplify the use of advanced features such as Docker containers, QEMU virtual machines, and IOU images. It also helps reduce the processing load on the host computer by running network devices inside the virtual machine. )
/Pg 45 0 R
/K [10]
>>
endobj
1234 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 430 0 R
/E <>
/Pg 45 0 R
/K [11]
>>
endobj
1235 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 430 0 R
/E (One of the main advantages of using the GNS3 VM is that it comes with several preinstalled tools, including Docker Engine. Docker Engine is a platform that allows users to download, run, and manage containers. In GNS3, these containers act as simple network devices for traffic generation. )
/Pg 45 0 R
/K [12]
>>
endobj
