/T (  After setting up the GNS3 environment and confirming that the GNS3 VM is running correctly, the next step is to add Docker containers. GNS3 provides an easy way to do this: users can either download preconfigured containers from Docker Hub \(a public repository with thousands of images\) or build their own. These containers are downloaded through the Docker Engine inside the GNS3 VM and can be reused later without needing to redownload them. )
/E (  After setting up the GNS3 environment and confirming that the GNS3 VM is running correctly, the next step is to add Docker containers. GNS3 provides an easy way to do this: users can either download preconfigured containers from Docker Hub \(a public repository with thousands of images\) or build their own. These containers are downloaded through the Docker Engine inside the GNS3 VM and can be reused later without needing to redownload them. )
/Pg 46 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
1244 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/P 431 0 R
/T (Docker containers can be managed and configured from the Docker Containers section in the GNS3 Preferences tab. Once added, these containers appear in the device list and can be used inside the network topology just like any other router, switch, or virtual machine. Each container acts as a network device and can exchange traffic with other components, such as routers or monitoring tools, inside the simulated environment. )
/E (Docker containers can be managed and configured from the Docker Containers section in the GNS3 Preferences tab. Once added, these containers appear in the device list and can be used inside the network topology just like any other router, switch, or virtual machine. Each container acts as a network device and can exchange traffic with other components, such as routers or monitoring tools, inside the simulated environment. )
/Pg 46 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
1245 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/P 431 0 R
/T (To ensure that the containers can communicate correctly inside the network topology, it is necessary to configure a static IP address for each one. This is done by modifying the network configuration file located at /etc/network/interfaces inside each container. The configuration  defines the IP address, subnet mask, default gateway, and DNS settings for the eth0  interface. A static IP ensures that the address remains the same, even after restarts, and it is valid  only within the GNS3 virtual network. The configuration used in each container is the same in  structure, with only the address field changing from one container to another to avoid IP  conflicts. This setting ensures reliable communication between devices.  Example of a static IP configuration for a configuration file:  # Static config for eth0  auto eth0  iface eth0 inet static  address 10.1.1.3  netmask 255.255.255.0  gateway 10.1.1.1  up echo nameserver 8.8.8.8 > /etc/resolv.conf   )
/E (To ensure that the containers can communicate correctly inside the network topology, it is necessary to configure a static IP address for each one. This is done by modifying the network configuration file located at /etc/network/interfaces inside each container. The configuration  defines the IP address, subnet mask, default gateway, and DNS settings for the eth0  interface. A static IP ensures that the address remains the same, even after restarts, and it is valid  only within the GNS3 virtual network. The configuration used in each container is the same in  structure, with only the address field changing from one container to another to avoid IP  conflicts. This setting ensures reliable communication between devices.  Example of a static IP configuration for a configuration file:  # Static config for eth0  auto eth0  iface eth0 inet static  address 10.1.1.3  netmask 255.255.255.0  gateway 10.1.1.1  up echo nameserver 8.8.8.8 > /etc/resolv.conf   )
/Pg 46 0 R
/K [8]
>>
endobj
