/T (  After scaling, the script transforms the continuous time series data into smaller sequences of 10 consecutive time steps, because the LSTM model works better with sequential input. For each sequence of 10 data points, the script predicts the next value of the incoming octets metric, which represents the amount of incoming traffic, so the model can learn how past traffic affects future traffic. These sequences are returned as two arrays: X containing the input sequences, and y containing the expected next values to predict. The function for this is shown in the script:  #creating)
/E (  After scaling, the script transforms the continuous time series data into smaller sequences of 10 consecutive time steps, because the LSTM model works better with sequential input. For each sequence of 10 data points, the script predicts the next value of the incoming octets metric, which represents the amount of incoming traffic, so the model can learn how past traffic affects future traffic. These sequences are returned as two arrays: X containing the input sequences, and y containing the expected next values to predict. The function for this is shown in the script:  #creating)
/Pg 58 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
