1817 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/P 451 0 R
/T (In the first few minutes after starting the system, the MSE is higher because the LSTM model has access to only a small amount of data \(less than 10 minutes\) and has not yet learned the full traffic pattern. However, as more data becomes available and the model retrains at every 60 seconds, it begins to make better predictions. After around 10 minutes of running, the MSE drops significantly, reaching very small values such as 0.00005, and this happens at the same time the prediction and real traffic lines begin to overlap \(Figure 17\). The graph confirms that once the model has enough historical data, it becomes more accurate and stable in predicting traffic. Table  2 shows how important prediction data is saved locally in a CSV file after each script run for historical analysis, debugging, and future improvement. It also used to compare results, generate reports, or retrain with saveddata. )
/E (In the first few minutes after starting the system, the MSE is higher because the LSTM model has access to only a small amount of data \(less than 10 minutes\) and has not yet learned the full traffic pattern. However, as more data becomes available and the model retrains at every 60 seconds, it begins to make better predictions. After around 10 minutes of running, the MSE drops significantly, reaching very small values such as 0.00005, and this happens at the same time the prediction and real traffic lines begin to overlap \(Figure 17\). The graph confirms that once the model has enough historical data, it becomes more accurate and stable in predicting traffic. Table  2 shows how important prediction data is saved locally in a CSV file after each script run for historical analysis, debugging, and future improvement. It also used to compare results, generate reports, or retrain with saveddata. )
/Pg 66 0 R
/K [12]
>>
