/Title (3.2 Traffic Prediction  Traffic Prediction refers to the process of forecasting future network traffic behavior, such as bandwidth usage, packet counts, or data rates, based on past measurements. It plays a crucial role in network planning, congestion control, and anomaly detection, allowing systems to adapt proactively rather than reactively. Inputs typically include historical traffic data, time features \(like hour or weekday\), and sometimes external variables such as service load or weather conditions [Zha22].)
/Dest [34 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 7 0 R
/Prev 5671 0 R
/Next 5675 0 R
>>
endobj
5674 0 obj
<<
/Title (In the first few minutes after starting the system, the MSE is higher because the LSTM model has access to only a small amount of data \(less than 10 minutes\) and has not yet learned the full traffic pattern. However, as more data becomes available and the model retrains at every 60 seconds, it begins to make better predictions. After around 10 minutes of running, the MSE drops significantly, reaching very small values such as 0.00005, and this happens at the same time the prediction and real traffic lines begin to overlap \(Figure 17\). The graph confirms that once the model has enough historical data, it becomes more accurate and stable in predicting traffic. Table  2 shows how important prediction data is saved locally in a CSV file after each script run for historical analysis, debugging, and future improvement. It also used to compare results, generate reports, or retrain with saveddata.)
/Dest [66 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 7 0 R
/Prev 5676 0 R
/First 5677 0 R
/Last 5678 0 R
/Count -2
/Next 5672 0 R
>>
endobj
5675 0 obj
<<
/Title (A traditional approach used in time-series forecasting is the Auto Regressive Integrated Moving Average \(ARIMA\) model, which combines autoregression and moving averages with differencing to handle trends. While ARIMA performs well on data with linear patterns and stationary behavior, its accuracy declines in complex, noisy environments like network traffic, where non-linearities are common.)
/Dest [34 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 7 0 R
/Prev 5673 0 R
/Next 5679 0 R
>>
endobj
