/Title (Grafana - while Prometheus is very good at collecting and storing data, it is not designed to present that data in a visual or user-friendly way. For this reason, we use Grafana, a powerful visualization tool that connects to Prometheus and displays the collected data in the form of graphs, tables, and interactive dashboards. Grafana queries Prometheus via its HTTP API, using Prometheus Query Language \(PromQL\) to extract specific metrics, such as network traffic, system load, memory usage, or error rates [Gra25]. These queries can be dynamically linked to visual panels that update in real time, providing a clear view of how the monitored system behaves. Grafana allows the user to build custom visual layouts, where each panel can display a different metric, aggregation, or transformation of the data. This enables the identification of patterns, bottlenecks, or anomalies that would be difficult to spot in raw numerical format. Moreover, Grafana supports alerting rules, thresholds, and filtering based on labels such as instance name, job, or interface, which adds a layer of intelligence to the monitoring setup. Grafana is typically deployed as a local or remote web application, accessible through a browser interface \(e.g., at http://localhost:3000\). It works by querying a connected Prometheus server, which collects time-series data from sources such as SNMP exporters or node exporters at regular intervals, often every 15 or 60 seconds. This setup allows Grafana to present up-to-date metrics in real time, offering users a clear and customizable way to monitor system performance, network activity, or infrastructure health through interactive dashboards [Gra25].)
/Dest [43 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 7 0 R
/Prev 5816 0 R
/Next 5820 0 R
>>
endobj
