/Title (To collect, store, and visualize the metrics generated within the network topology, a set of monitoring tools was used. These tools work together to provide real-time insights into both system performance and network activity, as well as to display results from AI scripts that detect anomalies and predict traffic. Three tools were used for this purpose: Grafana for vizualizantions, Prometheus and Pushgateway for scraping. All these are running on local host, each on a configurable port, but usually: 3000 - Grafana, 9090 - Prometheus and 9091 - Pushgateway.  This setup ensures that the tools are fully accessible without requiring an Internet connection. All of them are open-source and freely available for download from their official websites [Gra25; Pro25].)
/Dest [50 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 5931 0 R
/Next 5938 0 R
>>
endobj
5938 0 obj
<<
/Title (As the main data collector, Prometheus manages all incoming data before being visualized or analyzed. It works by periodically sending HTTP requests \(called scrapes\) to endpoints that expose metrics in a specific format. In this thesis, Prometheus collects data from two types of endpoints: Docker containers \(via Node Exporter\) and Cisco Router \(via SNMP\). This integration is achieved by specifying the endpoints \(targets\) in the Prometheus configuration file \(prometheus.yml\). Below is a snippet of the Prometheus configuration file used, written in YAML format. It defines how Prometheus collects metrics from Docker containers, the Pushgateway, and the Cisco router in the GNS3 topology:  global:)
/Dest [50 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 5931 0 R
/Prev 5937 0 R
>>
