/Title (Grafana also supports alerts, which are useful for keeping track of system health without having to watch the dashboard all the time. Users can set alerts to trigger if a specific metric goes above or below a certain limit. When that happens, Grafana can send a notification by email, chat, or other tools. This helps detect problems early and take action quickly, keeping the system stable and reliable.)
/Dest [53 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 7 0 R
/Prev 5952 0 R
/Next 5946 0 R
>>
endobj
5951 0 obj
<<
/Title (The first job, named alpine_nodes_forwarded, collects system metrics from Docker containers running inside the GNS3 network. Prometheus accesses these containers through the router's IP address \(192.168.0.112\) using different ports \(9201-9204\). Each container runs its own Node Exporter service, and this service listens to a unique por, that collects system-level metrics such as CPU usage, memory usage, disk space, and network activity and then exponses it. These metrics help monitor the health and performance of each container. Besides this, Prometheus exposes many other metrics from both router and containers, including CPU usage, memory usage, the amount of incoming traffic, and the status of each network interface.)
/Dest [51 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 7 0 R
/Prev 5949 0 R
/Next 5953 0 R
>>
endobj
5952 0 obj
<<
/Title (Grafana supports many types of charts and graphs, such as line graphs, bar charts, gauges, and tables. These visual elements are very customizable, so users can change colors, labels, sizes, and even the way data is grouped or filtered. This flexibility makes it easy to build dashboards that fit exactly what the user needs, whether it's for system monitoring, traffic analysis, or performance trends.)
/Dest [53 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 7 0 R
/Prev 5954 0 R
/Next 5950 0 R
>>
